{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfaTganrqjxCm5QvMYrtX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 08 is done, and the main result is a finding: **Chu & Stuckey's Theorem 1, the definite move, is false**, both as the paper states it and as `customer_search.c:313–330` and `Py:404–412` implement it. At a single search node it can throw away the last solution. The gate passes: `lake build` ok, still only the one baseline `sorry`, 1,300 tests passed.\n\n**The counterexample, proved in Lean** (`definiteMove_counterexample` in `Search/DefiniteMove.lean`):\n- **The graph:** 14 customers, 26 edges. At state `S = {2}` with `k = 6`, every premise of the code holds. Customer `0` is playable, `close = open = 3`, it is first in index order, and the Python port of the filter keeps only `0`.\n- **The failure:** a solution exists from `{2}`, but none from the child `{0,2,3,4}`. Lean checks the second half over the only 7 states reachable within 6 stacks.\n- **Why the paper's proof breaks:** customers 3 and 4 share one new stack. Closing them first gains two stacks for one; the proof counts them as \"extra stacks closed\" even when the solution had already closed them.\n- **A second slip in the proof:** its literal reordered sequence isn't a solution even where the theorem holds, because customers that `q` finishes stay open until their turn. The smallest case has 4 customers.\n\n**The repair, proved sound in Lean:**\n- **The rule:** with `b(T)` the number of open stacks after closing `T`, require `b(child) ≤ b(B)` for every state `B` between `S` and the child that avoids `q`. The code's test is only the case `B = S`.\n- **The proof:** it rests on `b` being submodular (`openStacks_submodular`).\n- **A cheap form to implement:** it suffices to match `open − 1` of the customers the move finishes to distinct new stacks. By Hall's theorem this matching form is equivalent to the repair; that equivalence is checked, not proved. In the solver it would cost one small bipartite matching per candidate. I did not change the solver.\n\n**What this means for the corpus:**\n- Every configuration that produced a refutation runs the unsound form, so the soundness claim in §2.9 is false as stated wherever the definite move is on.\n- No wrong `unsat` on a whole instance has been found: none on 600,000 random graphs at 10–18 customers, none on 90,000 targeted graphs at 12–17, and both counterexample instances still get the right answer.\n- Whether a whole instance can ever be falsely refuted is open. Item 12 has to either prove its theorem for the repaired rule or name this gap.\n\n**Checks:**\n- **Where losses appear:** under the code's rule there is no loss on any graph up to 9 vertices (checked exhaustively) or on sparse connected graphs on 10. Losses appear only at 14–16 vertices, in a family of graphs shaped like the failed proof. Deleting vertices and edges never got a counterexample below 14.\n- **The repair:** zero losses in 2.05 G checks, plus 272 M more on the targeted family.\n- **Python:** `python -m paper2.search_check --definite` runs in 16 s on 36,911 graphs with zero failures, and there are 7 new tests, including one that swaps the code's test in for the repair and checks it gets caught.\n- **Docs:** `paper2/search_soundness.md` has a finding box at the top, the finding and repair in §2.2, §2.9 marked false as stated, and a new §4.2.\n- **Search tools:** the C search and generator that found the counterexamples are added as `paper2/definite_hunt.c` and `paper2/definite_hunt_gen.py`.\n\nPROGRESS.md now reads 8/13 and item 08 is checked off. I suggested that item 09 check the subset rule the same way before stating it: the definite move also passed item 06's checks up to 7 customers and only failed at 14. Nothing is committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":618,"cache_read_inpu